# Discord bot credentials — get these from https://discord.com/developers/applications
# 1. Create an application → "Bot" tab → "Reset Token" to get DISCORD_TOKEN.
# 2. On the "General Information" tab, copy the "App ID" → DISCORD_CLIENT_ID.
# 3. Under "Bot → Privileged Gateway Intents", enable:
#    - MESSAGE CONTENT INTENT (required for the spawn catch system)

# ─── Shared settings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which environment to use: "development" or "production".
# The bot token + client ID are loaded from .env.{NODE_ENV} (see below).
NODE_ENV=development

# Optional: set PORT or HEALTH_CHECK_PORT to enable the HTTP health check endpoint
# (useful for hosting platforms like Railway, Render, Fly.io).
# PORT=3000

# Optional: set LOG_LEVEL=debug for verbose output (default: info).
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Optional: set DB_PATH to override the SQLite database file location.
# Defaults to ./data/brainrot.db
# DB_PATH=/path/to/brainrot.db

# Roblox API key (optional but recommended for username resolution).
# Get one at https://create.roblox.com/docs/apis/authentication
# ROBLOX_API_KEY=your-roblox-api-key-here

# ─── Environment-specific credentials ────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy .env.development.example → .env.development  (for dev bot)
# Copy .env.production.example  → .env.production   (for public/production bot)
# Only the file matching NODE_ENV above will be loaded for bot credentials.

# ─── For Docker ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# When running via `docker compose`, you can optionally list the environment-
# specific file in the `env_file` directive in docker-compose.yml, e.g.:
#   env_file:
#     - .env
#     - .env.production
# Or simply set DISCORD_TOKEN + DISCORD_CLIENT_ID directly in this .env file
# and leave NODE_ENV as-is.
